1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,340
6:15 in the morning.

2
00:00:01,860 --> 00:00:03,420
Your alarm goes off, and you tap the

3
00:00:03,420 --> 00:00:04,820
snooze button the way you always do.

4
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:07,000
The phone goes through the nightstand.

5
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:08,580
Then through the floor.

6
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:10,960
You hear it land in your downstairs neighbour's

7
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,160
kitchen, still ringing.

8
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:13,920
You sit up.

9
00:00:14,360 --> 00:00:15,680
Something feels cold.

10
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:17,080
You touch your head.

11
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:18,600
There's nothing there.

12
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:19,920
Not a single hair.

13
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:23,620
Sometime during the night, you got the power

14
00:00:23,620 --> 00:00:24,320
of Saitama.

15
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:25,740
One-Punch Man.

16
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:29,120
No transformation, no special moves, no gears, no

17
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:29,720
glowing aura.

18
00:00:30,540 --> 00:00:31,320
Just this:

19
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,860
anything you hit with real intent goes down

20
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:35,440
in one punch.

21
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:36,640
Anything.

22
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:39,780
Within a few months you'll be the strongest

23
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:41,240
person who has ever lived.

24
00:00:41,740 --> 00:00:43,180
And you'll find out that the hardest thing

25
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:45,000
about being unbeatable isn't the fighting.

26
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:46,780
It's everything after.

27
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:48,660
First problem:

28
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:51,100
everything in your apartment is made of paper

29
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:51,420
now.

30
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,980
You squeeze the toothpaste and it empties in

31
00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:54,440
one shot.

32
00:00:54,980 --> 00:00:56,260
You turn the tap and it comes off

33
00:00:56,260 --> 00:00:56,840
in your hand.

34
00:00:57,560 --> 00:00:59,220
You pick up a mug and you're holding

35
00:00:59,220 --> 00:00:59,700
a handle.

36
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:08,720
So you learn rule one, the way Saitama

37
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:11,200
lives it: your strength doesn't turn off.

38
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:11,980
You do.

39
00:01:12,480 --> 00:01:13,320
You move slowly.

40
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,160
You hold things like they're eggs.

41
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:17,600
Door handles get one finger.

42
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:20,080
By lunchtime you can make a cup of

43
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:22,740
tea without destroying the kettle, and you have

44
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:24,280
never been prouder of anything.

45
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:25,940
And the hair.

46
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:28,680
Real stress hair loss takes two to four

47
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:30,280
months to show up, and it usually grows

48
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:30,640
back.

49
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:32,500
Yours took one night.

50
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:34,480
And it's not coming back.

51
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,260
You need to know how strong you are,

52
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:39,180
so you go to the quietest gym in

53
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:40,800
town and ask to use the heavy bag.

54
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:41,960
The trainer laughs.

55
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:43,220
You tap it.

56
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:43,820
Barely.

57
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:45,920
The bag doesn't swing.

58
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:49,140
It tears off the chain, goes through the

59
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:51,400
wall, and lands in the car park outside.

60
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,340
So you learn rule two.

61
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:56,280
With Saitama there are two kinds of punches:

62
00:01:56,780 --> 00:01:58,800
the normal one, which is already too much,

63
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:01,340
and the serious one, which you never want

64
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:02,400
to find out about indoors.

65
00:02:03,580 --> 00:02:05,760
You go home and practise holding back on

66
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:06,160
a pillow.

67
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:07,820
Then on a mattress.

68
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:09,660
Then you buy a new mattress.

69
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:13,500
On day five you test your speed somewhere

70
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,100
safe: an empty field, nobody around.

71
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:17,300
You punch the air.

72
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:18,320
Not at anything.

73
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:19,000
Just the air.

74
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:21,820
There's a crack like a gunshot, and every

75
00:02:21,820 --> 00:02:23,520
car alarm in the street behind you goes

76
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:23,800
off.

77
00:02:24,980 --> 00:02:27,380
The fastest punches ever measured in a lab

78
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:29,460
hit about 12 metres per second.

79
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:32,120
To make that crack, your fist broke the

80
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:35,740
sound barrier, around 343 metres per second.

81
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:39,070
That's nearly thirty times faster, and about eight

82
00:02:39,070 --> 00:02:40,400
hundred times the energy.

83
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:44,320
You punched the sky, and the sky complained.

84
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:46,800
Somebody filmed it from a balcony.

85
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:50,720
By day nine the video has twelve million

86
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:51,200
views.

87
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:53,500
The comments are split right down the middle.

88
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:55,200
Half of them say it's CGI.

89
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:58,280
The other half say it's CGI, but good

90
00:02:58,280 --> 00:02:58,620
CGI.

91
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:00,280
Nobody believes you.

92
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:01,600
That's the strange part.

93
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,000
You can stop a truck, but you can't

94
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,040
stop people saying you're fake.

95
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:08,540
You find out that being strong in real

96
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:10,120
life doesn't come with an audience.

97
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:12,760
It comes with a landlord asking who is

98
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,600
paying for the wall.

99
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:16,540
On day twelve you try to live a

100
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:17,120
normal life.

101
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:18,680
You have a job interview at nine.

102
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:20,860
The interviewer reaches out to shake your hand,

103
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:23,640
and you spend the longest second of your

104
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:25,880
life deciding how hard a handshake is.

105
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:27,780
You go with "dead fish".

106
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:29,280
He winces anyway.

107
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:30,900
Then the coffee machine jams,

108
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:33,600
everyone sighs, and you give it a little

109
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:34,180
tap to help.

110
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:35,440
It works again.

111
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:37,560
It's also now part of the wall.

112
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:38,720
You don't get the job.

113
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:40,820
On the way out, the security guard asks

114
00:03:40,820 --> 00:03:41,360
for a selfie.

115
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:53,540
On day seventeen you're walking home from the

116
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:55,940
supermarket when a crane on a building site

117
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:56,520
snaps.

118
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:58,660
A load of steel beams is falling toward

119
00:03:58,660 --> 00:03:59,860
a bus stop full of people.

120
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:01,580
You don't think.

121
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:02,440
You jump.

122
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,060
You catch the first beam, punch the second

123
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:07,280
one sideways into the empty lot, and set

124
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:08,840
the third one down on the pavement like

125
00:04:08,840 --> 00:04:09,160
a sofa.

126
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:10,340
Nobody is hurt.

127
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:12,420
It takes about four seconds.

128
00:04:13,780 --> 00:04:15,520
When the news arrives, a man in a

129
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:17,800
shiny costume who arrived ten minutes later is

130
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:18,520
giving interviews.

131
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:20,520
You're holding a bag of groceries with the

132
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:21,500
eggs still intact.

133
00:04:24,460 --> 00:04:25,500
You decide that counts as a win.

134
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:28,840
After the crane, your oldest friend shows up

135
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:30,500
at your door at five in the morning

136
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:31,660
in running gear.

137
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:33,240
"Teach me," they say.

138
00:04:33,640 --> 00:04:34,040
"The routine.

139
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:36,680
A hundred push-ups, a hundred sit-ups, a hundred

140
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:38,940
squats, ten kilometres.

141
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,160
Every day." So you train together.

142
00:04:41,960 --> 00:04:44,200
And here's the thing: in real life, that

143
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:45,500
routine works,

144
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:46,580
for a while.

145
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:49,040
You get fitter fast in the first weeks,

146
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:50,680
then the progress slows down,

147
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:53,360
and months of the same hundred push-ups is

148
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:54,460
how a lot of people end up with

149
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:55,640
sore elbows and shoulders.

150
00:04:56,420 --> 00:04:58,280
After three weeks your friend can run ten

151
00:04:58,280 --> 00:04:59,400
kilometres without stopping.

152
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:00,300
They're proud.

153
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:01,220
They should be.

154
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:02,780
They still can't lift a car.

155
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:05,360
They ask what your secret really is.

156
00:05:05,360 --> 00:05:06,720
You honestly don't know.

157
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:10,100
On day thirty, two people in grey suits

158
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:10,780
knock on your door.

159
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:11,720
Very polite.

160
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:13,620
They'd like to run a few tests.

161
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:16,220
They bring a machine that measures punching force.

162
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:17,380
You hit it gently.

163
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:18,800
It stops working.

164
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:20,380
They bring a bigger one.

165
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:21,300
Same thing.

166
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:23,480
They bring a block of steel the size

167
00:05:23,480 --> 00:05:25,520
of a fridge, and it folds like a

168
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:26,000
napkin.

169
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,520
At the end of the day they don't

170
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:28,960
have a number.

171
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:30,640
They have a report with one line:

172
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:32,860
"Upper limit: not found."

173
00:05:40,580 --> 00:05:41,400
And you notice something.

174
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:42,800
You're not nervous.

175
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:44,680
You're not even curious.

176
00:05:45,620 --> 00:05:47,800
You're checking the time, because the supermarket has

177
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:48,860
a sale at six.

178
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:50,920
Word gets around.

179
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:53,080
Fighters start showing up.

180
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:54,680
Boxers, wrestlers,

181
00:05:55,380 --> 00:05:56,820
a man who has trained in the mountains

182
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:57,980
for twelve years.

183
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:00,080
Each of them gets one tap.

184
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:02,400
You start apologising before you even swing.

185
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:05,000
One of them asks you to fight seriously,

186
00:06:05,460 --> 00:06:05,960
just once.

187
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:07,140
You can't.

188
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:09,200
If you did, there would be no one

189
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:09,760
left to ask.

190
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:12,980
By day sixty you've stopped trying to prove

191
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:13,460
anything.

192
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:14,540
You just help.

193
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:17,080
A car stuck in a flooded underpass at

194
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,460
night: you carry it out, with the family

195
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:21,360
still inside, and set it down on dry

196
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:21,560
road.

197
00:06:21,560 --> 00:06:23,760
A runaway shopping trolley heading for a busy

198
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:24,140
street:

199
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:25,180
one finger.

200
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:28,320
A cat on a roof: you jump, very

201
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:28,600
gently.

202
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:30,060
Nobody films those ones.

203
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:31,540
Nobody writes about them.

204
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:33,560
A little kid in a window waves at

205
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:35,620
you, and you wave back, and that's the

206
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:36,140
whole reward.

207
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:38,060
It should feel like enough.

208
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:39,640
Some nights it does.

209
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:42,400
This is the part nobody warns you about.

210
00:06:42,820 --> 00:06:44,180
At first, winning felt amazing.

211
00:06:44,460 --> 00:06:45,440
Then it felt normal.

212
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:47,120
Then it felt like nothing.

213
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,900
Psychologists call it hedonic adaptation: we get used

214
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:53,240
to almost anything, good or bad, and the

215
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:53,720
thrill fades.

216
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:59,200
Saitama's real enemy was never a monster.

217
00:06:59,780 --> 00:07:00,520
It was boredom.

218
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:03,800
Every fight ends before it starts.

219
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:06,040
Every video game feels slow.

220
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:08,720
Every danger looks like it's standing still.

221
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:11,400
You'd give anything for one opponent who makes

222
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:13,640
you sweat, just once, to remember what it

223
00:07:13,640 --> 00:07:14,300
felt like to try.

224
00:07:15,080 --> 00:07:17,580
You stop setting goals, because every goal is

225
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:18,200
already done.

226
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:20,700
You stop telling people about your day, because

227
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:21,920
your day is always the same:

228
00:07:22,580 --> 00:07:24,740
something happens, you hit it once, it's over.

229
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:27,680
The strongest person in the world, and the

230
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,420
thing you miss most is losing.

231
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:33,140
One night, just to feel something, you go

232
00:07:33,140 --> 00:07:34,880
to an empty beach and jump.

233
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:36,080
Not a careful jump.

234
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:37,100
A real one.

235
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:38,840
The ground cracks.

236
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:41,120
The clouds go past like you're in an

237
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:41,500
elevator.

238
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:45,180
Then the sky turns black, and below you

239
00:07:45,240 --> 00:07:47,560
there's the curve of the Earth, blue and

240
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:48,700
white and silent.

241
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:51,060
To leave Earth for good you'd need about

242
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:52,520
eleven kilometres per second.

243
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:54,080
You don't know if you went that fast.

244
00:07:54,720 --> 00:07:56,640
You know you're falling back, and for about

245
00:07:56,640 --> 00:07:59,780
twenty seconds, falling feels like the most exciting

246
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:01,720
thing that has happened to you in months.

247
00:08:02,560 --> 00:08:03,420
You land in the ocean.

248
00:08:03,980 --> 00:08:05,440
It takes you an hour to swim home.

249
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,140
You smile the whole way.

250
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:11,000
And then, on day one hundred and twenty,

251
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,440
the sky turns red.

252
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:15,340
Something is coming in from space, a rock

253
00:08:15,340 --> 00:08:18,020
the size of a stadium, and every government

254
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:19,880
on Earth runs out of time at the

255
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:20,580
same moment.

256
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:22,100
For the first time in months,

257
00:08:22,740 --> 00:08:23,640
your heart is pounding.

258
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:26,740
Millions of people are looking up, and for

259
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:28,140
once, so are you.

260
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:29,660
Not because you're scared.

261
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:33,720
Because, finally, something is coming that might actually

262
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:34,400
be a fight.

263
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:35,780
You jump.

264
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:38,360
You meet it above the clouds, and for

265
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:40,620
one second you throw a serious punch.

266
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:43,260
The rock turns into gravel and dust.

267
00:08:43,260 --> 00:08:45,760
A few pieces still rain down on the

268
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:46,000
city.

269
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:48,640
A few roofs get holes, a few cars

270
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:49,220
get dents.

271
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:50,700
Nobody dies.

272
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:15,000
The next morning a headline asks who will

273
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:15,860
pay for the damage.

274
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:18,500
So you do what Saitama does.

275
00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:19,700
You go shopping.

276
00:09:20,340 --> 00:09:20,980
Eggs are half price,

277
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:23,600
and for some reason that makes you happier

278
00:09:23,600 --> 00:09:24,860
than saving the planet did.

279
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:37,040
That night, you set your alarm for 6:15.

280
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:38,960
In the morning it rings, and you reach

281
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:40,220
out and tap it.

282
00:09:40,540 --> 00:09:40,800
Gently.

283
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:42,220
It stops ringing.

284
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:44,180
The nightstand is still there.

285
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,340
Your friend texts: "Run tomorrow?

286
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:48,480
Ten kilometres." You say yes.

287
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:50,020
Not because you need it.

288
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:52,220
Because it's the one thing left that you

289
00:09:52,220 --> 00:09:53,280
get to do with someone else.

290
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:55,800
It's the hardest thing you've learned all year.

291
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:59,520
So: would you take one punch that wins

292
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:02,220
every fight, if it meant nothing would ever

293
00:10:02,220 --> 00:10:03,140
feel hard again?

294
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:05,400
And what's the first thing you'd punch?

295
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:06,720
Tell us in the comments.
